1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
They're where the rubber meets the road.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,000
The crucial interface between land and sky.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
They're the main drag at big city airports.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
And a thin ribbon of hope in the bush.

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
They can be three miles long or as compact as a football field.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Now, Runways on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
The runway.

8
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:51,380
It just might be the most underappreciated piece of infrastructure in America.

9
00:00:52,820 --> 00:00:55,280
Bridges and tunnels get all the oohs and odds,

10
00:00:55,860 --> 00:00:58,460
but runways are the backbone of the aviation industry,

11
00:00:58,461 --> 00:01:02,020
which is the backbone of the transportation industry.

12
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:12,100
Every day, 175,000 aircraft take off and touch down on runways in the U.S.

13
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:15,720
Everything sort of revolves around the runway.

14
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:23,080
Every pilot that goes out there trusts the airport proprietor, air traffic control,

15
00:01:23,081 --> 00:01:29,760
that they have 9,400 feet of absolutely the safest runway that we can provide for them.

16
00:01:30,340 --> 00:01:33,160
Runways aren't really the most glamorous of structures, certainly,

17
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:37,540
but they do form the basis of the air transportation system.

18
00:01:37,541 --> 00:01:41,660
I mean, you really have to get down and get up from somewhere.

19
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:43,620
So they are, of course, very important.

20
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:48,260
I've been flying for 20 years, and the first thing you have to learn

21
00:01:48,261 --> 00:01:50,980
is how to take it off and how to put it back on the ground.

22
00:01:50,981 --> 00:01:57,920
All the rest of your mission you learn later, but if you can't get it in the air and get it on the ground, you can't go any further than that.

23
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:02,160
Before we go any further, let's make one thing perfectly clear.

24
00:02:02,940 --> 00:02:08,560
Runways are never under any circumstances to be confused with ordinary roads, or even the interstate.

25
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:15,900
In terms of layout, construction, and design, the runway is to the interstate, as the interstate is to an urban sidewalk.

26
00:02:15,901 --> 00:02:24,300
And for the same reason, sidewalk, highway, and runway each have to handle progressively greater loads of stress.

27
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:29,140
The runway must accept tremendous forces on it.

28
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:40,220
The landing of a 500,000-pound aircraft going in excess of 140 miles an hour puts a tremendous strain on the runway's structural limitations.

29
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:42,800
So the runway has to be elastic.

30
00:02:42,801 --> 00:02:52,680
It has to be built in such a way that can accept this huge weight and accept the heat that's generated by the stopping aircraft and not the form.

31
00:02:53,140 --> 00:02:54,640
So it's very complicated.

32
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:59,080
The complications begin in the planning stage.

33
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:06,460
Before any paving is poured, a host of ground, subsoil, line of sight and wind studies must be conducted.

34
00:03:06,461 --> 00:03:13,180
planes need wind to take off and land into and surveyors have to draw up a wind rose

35
00:03:13,181 --> 00:03:16,720
a map of wind patterns that will dictate the layout of the airstrip

36
00:03:16,721 --> 00:03:23,080
a precise assessment of surrounding terrain including mountains and skyscrapers is also

37
00:03:23,081 --> 00:03:27,580
critical to make sure that planes will have room to clear such obstacles in time

38
00:03:27,581 --> 00:03:34,420
runways are made of either concrete which lasts longer and has less give

39
00:03:34,421 --> 00:03:38,000
or asphalt, which is cheaper but requires more maintenance.

40
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:43,240
Deciding what kind of finish to put on the asphalt or concrete is a crucial call.

41
00:03:45,380 --> 00:03:50,900
That's because runway slickness, caused by everything from rain, snow, and ice, to oil,

42
00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:55,680
to tire skid marks in the touchdown zone, is a huge concern for everyone,

43
00:03:55,900 --> 00:03:59,360
from tire and plane manufacturers to NASA to the FAA,

44
00:03:59,900 --> 00:04:03,440
all of whom have conducted extensive runway weather and skid tests.

45
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:10,120
If you think driving the freeway in a storm is dicey, imagine breaking through dense clouds

46
00:04:10,121 --> 00:04:17,920
about 10 seconds before hitting a snow-covered runway at 160 miles an hour.

47
00:04:17,921 --> 00:04:23,380
This hydraulic sled track at NASA's Aircraft Landing Dynamics Facility in Hampton, Virginia,

48
00:04:23,381 --> 00:04:28,300
is used to simulate high-speed landings in torrential rainfall of up to 40 inches an

49
00:04:28,301 --> 00:04:29,300
hour.

50
00:04:29,301 --> 00:04:36,400
It was here in the 1960s that researchers came up with an invaluable weapon in the fight

51
00:04:36,401 --> 00:04:40,480
against runway skid.

52
00:04:40,481 --> 00:04:42,540
Grooving.

53
00:04:42,541 --> 00:04:46,900
Creating narrow runoff canals in the paving through which water can drain is a simple

54
00:04:46,901 --> 00:04:50,940
but revolutionary idea that has changed the look and texture of runways.

55
00:04:50,941 --> 00:04:58,940
As the airplane operating speeds increased, they couldn't get rid of the water fast enough

56
00:04:58,941 --> 00:05:03,400
and hence there would be a pressure buildup that would lift the tire off the pavement,

57
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:05,300
much like a water skier.

58
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:12,600
And we felt that if we added channels to get the water off of the runway quicker,

59
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:14,480
that would help solve the problem.

60
00:05:15,580 --> 00:05:19,020
Grooving is now used on 800 runways in the U.S.

61
00:05:19,021 --> 00:05:22,100
and on thousands of miles of highway throughout the country.

62
00:05:22,101 --> 00:05:29,900
In case grooving fails, some airports, especially those with runways that abut large bodies

63
00:05:29,901 --> 00:05:36,500
of water, have adopted a last line of defense against runway skin.

64
00:05:36,501 --> 00:05:42,740
This styrofoam-like bed developed by the FAA was installed in 1998 to keep planes at New

65
00:05:42,741 --> 00:05:48,860
York's LaGuardia from sliding into Flushing Bay.

66
00:05:48,861 --> 00:05:52,260
Clearly, runways have come a long way since the early days of aviation,

67
00:05:52,680 --> 00:05:57,260
when planes were so light that they could touch down and rumble to a stop on farms and meadows.

68
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:04,420
The typical runway has actually evolved from a grass field,

69
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:08,740
and if they were really pressing for efficiency, they mowed the grass.

70
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:11,980
But sooner or later, as aircraft evolved,

71
00:06:12,660 --> 00:06:16,960
they found that the grass caused additional drag on the wheels of the airplanes,

72
00:06:16,961 --> 00:06:20,140
so they went to bare dirt surfaces.

73
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:24,000
Dirt was better as long as the skies were clear,

74
00:06:24,220 --> 00:06:27,560
but landing on dirt in a heavy rain was a risky proposition.

75
00:06:28,280 --> 00:06:31,480
If you were the unfortunate victim that was the first lander

76
00:06:31,481 --> 00:06:33,900
and you didn't know what the condition of the field was

77
00:06:33,901 --> 00:06:38,080
and your wheels sunk into mud up to the hubs, you know,

78
00:06:38,120 --> 00:06:40,520
when you were still going 40 or 60 miles an hour,

79
00:06:40,521 --> 00:06:42,080
it could really be disastrous.

80
00:06:43,440 --> 00:06:45,440
The next logical step was the paved runway,

81
00:06:45,441 --> 00:06:47,780
which was little more than a paved highway

82
00:06:47,781 --> 00:06:50,660
and first appeared at the end of the 1920s.

83
00:06:51,400 --> 00:06:54,760
Made of concrete, this revolutionary advance enabled pilots

84
00:06:54,761 --> 00:06:58,280
to make all-weather takeoffs and landings at any time of day or night.

85
00:07:00,340 --> 00:07:03,900
Until the 1930s, many smaller airlines landed on water.

86
00:07:04,980 --> 00:07:07,962
One of the first passenger airlines was a seaplane service in St.

87
00:07:07,963 --> 00:07:08,880
Petersburg, Florida.

88
00:07:09,260 --> 00:07:12,900
Flying boats, as they were called, had a smooth, reinforced underbelly

89
00:07:12,901 --> 00:07:16,320
and needed just a few hundred feet of calm sea to land and dock.

90
00:07:17,340 --> 00:07:22,120
But landing on water, in effect, a moving runway, was full of hazards,

91
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:26,780
and many lives were lost in the 30s when pilots dipped their wings into high waves

92
00:07:26,781 --> 00:07:28,280
and tumbled to destruction.

93
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:33,980
By the end of the decade, water had given way almost entirely to concrete.

94
00:07:35,360 --> 00:07:39,100
LaGuardia, the first major airport in the U.S., was built in 1939

95
00:07:39,101 --> 00:07:43,460
and boasted two landmark runways, each nearly a mile long.

96
00:07:45,380 --> 00:07:51,080
But even that wasn't long enough for the new generation of bigger, heavier planes spawned by World War II

97
00:07:51,081 --> 00:07:58,460
and the ascendancy of jets in the 1950s and 60s, which doubled the weight of previous planes.

98
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:02,380
Overnight, runways too would have to double in size.

99
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:05,480
Jet Age runways for the new Jet Age.

100
00:08:05,481 --> 00:08:14,880
The jet age of the 1960s turned air traffic into mass traffic

101
00:08:14,881 --> 00:08:17,900
and triggered a revolution in airport and runway design.

102
00:08:19,020 --> 00:08:22,180
Dulles International was the first jet age airport in the U.S.

103
00:08:22,760 --> 00:08:27,080
Cleared for takeoffs in 1962, Dulles boasted three runways,

104
00:08:27,081 --> 00:08:30,360
two of which stretched out an unprecedented two miles.

105
00:08:31,740 --> 00:08:34,580
The 1960s were a boom period for runway construction.

106
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:37,500
Old runways had to be extended and widened,

107
00:08:37,700 --> 00:08:40,580
and new runways had to be built to handle the increase in traffic

108
00:08:40,581 --> 00:08:43,080
and the immense stresses of the latest jets.

109
00:08:45,420 --> 00:08:48,420
Laying down a thick coat of concrete for a two-mile runway

110
00:08:48,421 --> 00:08:51,640
that was, with shoulders, nearly as wide as a football field

111
00:08:51,641 --> 00:08:53,060
was no small task.

112
00:08:54,900 --> 00:08:58,080
And runway contractors were quick to adopt slip-form paving,

113
00:08:58,520 --> 00:09:01,100
a streamlined technique developed by the highway industry

114
00:09:01,101 --> 00:09:03,460
as the preferred means of tackling the job.

115
00:09:04,580 --> 00:09:07,058
With slip form, paving is continuous.

116
00:09:07,059 --> 00:09:10,340
The wet concrete is dumped in front of the paving

117
00:09:10,341 --> 00:09:15,640
apparatus where it is churned and then shaped into a solid slab a few yards away in the

118
00:09:15,641 --> 00:09:23,960
rear. The paver moves a foot and a half a minute, or about 10,000 feet in five days.

119
00:09:23,961 --> 00:09:30,180
Keeping the process going is all important since any stop risks a bump in the runway.

120
00:09:30,181 --> 00:09:33,140
This slip form uses a very stiff mix of concrete.

121
00:09:33,141 --> 00:09:39,360
The paving can be laid out without fixed side forms to hold the wet slab in place.

122
00:09:39,361 --> 00:09:45,680
This runway, constructed in Atlanta's Municipal Airport in 1969, was laid out in 25-foot-wide,

123
00:09:45,681 --> 00:09:48,760
two-mile-long strips.

124
00:09:48,761 --> 00:09:52,900
Six strips were laid side to side to complete the job.

125
00:09:52,901 --> 00:09:58,300
Unlike today's runway mixes, which can be ready for traffic in one to three days, 1960s

126
00:09:58,301 --> 00:10:03,640
concrete usually took two weeks to set. In order to toughen the runway for the

127
00:10:03,641 --> 00:10:07,520
punishment to come, a steel mesh was placed between eight-inch layers of

128
00:10:07,521 --> 00:10:13,720
concrete. The mesh spreads the stress loads of landing craft. Dowel bars were

129
00:10:13,721 --> 00:10:18,420
also strategically placed to connect adjacent slabs.

130
00:10:19,640 --> 00:10:25,320
The 21st century so far is still the age of jets and runways today are

131
00:10:25,321 --> 00:10:30,260
constructed in much the same way as in the 1960s and to the same general specifications.

132
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:36,300
They range in thickness from 6 to 50 inches, depending on the weight and intensity of the

133
00:10:36,301 --> 00:10:40,498
traffic they'll have to bear. But they are not evenly thick throughout.

134
00:10:40,499 --> 00:10:41,680
The biggest loading,

135
00:10:41,880 --> 00:10:47,949
of course, is in the touchdown areas, roughly 2,000 feet on either end of the runway.

136
00:10:47,950 --> 00:10:48,650
That can

137
00:10:48,651 --> 00:10:54,660
amount to several hundred thousand pounds in a relatively small tire contact area.

138
00:10:55,320 --> 00:11:00,520
and hence they normally have the thickness of the pavement in those touchdown areas

139
00:11:00,521 --> 00:11:04,180
greater than what they have in the middle portion.

140
00:11:05,980 --> 00:11:09,120
Creating a smooth grade is critical in runway construction

141
00:11:09,121 --> 00:11:14,580
since planes landing at high speeds will turn even minor variations into a roller coaster ride.

142
00:11:15,280 --> 00:11:19,220
The string line technique, in which surveyors set up a line on either side of the paver

143
00:11:19,221 --> 00:11:22,060
to trace the desired grade of the runway, is the most popular.

144
00:11:22,061 --> 00:11:28,960
A wand from the paver touches the line, measuring changes in elevation, and the machine hydraulically

145
00:11:28,961 --> 00:11:33,300
adjusts the height of its paving pan to make the smoothest, most even surface possible.

146
00:11:34,860 --> 00:11:40,480
A smooth runway is just one aspect of what makes a safe runway, and runway safety is

147
00:11:40,481 --> 00:11:45,400
an enormous concern, especially after construction, when the runway is running.

148
00:11:45,401 --> 00:11:54,440
That's because, ironically, the time a plane spends on the ground can be one of the most

149
00:11:54,441 --> 00:11:57,120
dangerous parts of air travel.

150
00:11:57,121 --> 00:12:00,988
Runway collisions between planes have killed 49 people in the U.S.

151
00:12:00,989 --> 00:12:02,160
since 1990, although

152
00:12:02,161 --> 00:12:05,820
only six since early 1991.

153
00:12:05,821 --> 00:12:10,580
And incursions, violations of runway space by aircraft and other vehicles, hit an all-time

154
00:12:10,581 --> 00:12:13,920
high of 431 in 2000.

155
00:12:13,921 --> 00:12:16,920
There were 383 such incidents in 2001.

156
00:12:18,920 --> 00:12:26,920
In fact, the worst commercial crash in aviation history was caused by a runway smash-up in Tenerife in the Canary Islands in 1977,

157
00:12:26,921 --> 00:12:31,920
when two 747s collided on takeoff, killing 583 people.

158
00:12:32,920 --> 00:12:38,920
There are some airports that have what have clearly become known as hot spots,

159
00:12:38,921 --> 00:12:45,180
where repeated incursions, whether they be pilot deviations where a pilot made an error,

160
00:12:45,640 --> 00:12:51,960
or a vehicle making a runway incursion or going someplace on the airport where they're not supposed to.

161
00:12:52,620 --> 00:12:57,820
Those hot spots have been identified on those airports, made a matter of record,

162
00:12:58,280 --> 00:13:04,060
and the FAA and the airport proprietors work really hard as a team to fix those areas.

163
00:13:04,720 --> 00:13:07,620
Human error is the main cause of runway incursions,

164
00:13:07,621 --> 00:13:16,820
And since the mid-90s, both the FAA and NASA have been developing crash warning systems designed to keep pilots on top of airfield traffic.

165
00:13:18,640 --> 00:13:23,660
Like this advanced cockpit display system created at NASA's Langley Research Center.

166
00:13:23,661 --> 00:13:33,100
The system uses GPS signals, traffic sensors and NASA software to create an electronic

167
00:13:33,101 --> 00:13:38,120
moving map that shows and sounds an alert whenever an incursion is about to take place.

168
00:13:45,560 --> 00:13:50,580
Another serious but sometimes subtle danger on runways comes in the form of rubbish,

169
00:13:50,581 --> 00:13:55,089
or FOD, which is the acronym for foreign objects and debris.

170
00:13:55,090 --> 00:13:58,020
Rubbish, chunks of asphalt or concrete,

171
00:13:58,480 --> 00:14:01,580
loose bits of rubber can all wreak havoc in the engines of a jet.

172
00:14:02,740 --> 00:14:07,840
FOD has been a factor in 29 accidents in the U.S. since 1990, seven of them deadly.

173
00:14:12,220 --> 00:14:17,180
It was FOD in the form of a 16-inch piece of metal that had fallen off another plane

174
00:14:17,181 --> 00:14:21,440
that caused the supersonic Concorde to crash outside Paris in 2000,

175
00:14:21,980 --> 00:14:24,320
killing all 109 passengers and crew.

176
00:14:24,800 --> 00:14:27,040
The metal punctured the Concorde's front tire,

177
00:14:27,540 --> 00:14:31,260
firing heavy chunks of rubber into the jet's fuel tanks, which ignited.

178
00:14:33,700 --> 00:14:36,700
With our jet engines pushing through so much air,

179
00:14:36,800 --> 00:14:38,500
they act like vacuum cleaners on the front.

180
00:14:38,980 --> 00:14:42,060
And if your jet engine is mounted on your jet so that it's low enough,

181
00:14:42,061 --> 00:14:46,760
it can just pick a rock right up off the ground and suck it through the engine.

182
00:14:47,180 --> 00:14:49,260
And that in itself causes a lot of damage.

183
00:14:50,080 --> 00:14:55,920
Objects must be attended to at all times at an airport, day and night,

184
00:14:55,921 --> 00:14:59,520
because, frankly, the pilot cannot see it when he lands.

185
00:14:59,980 --> 00:15:04,140
It is simply too small, and the landing evolution is too quick

186
00:15:04,141 --> 00:15:07,420
for a pilot to be able to discern objects on the runway.

187
00:15:08,580 --> 00:15:13,220
Many airports use special suction vehicles that can pick up pebbles and loose turf

188
00:15:13,221 --> 00:15:14,820
where they conduct pod sweeps.

189
00:15:17,180 --> 00:15:21,180
In between, inspectors make daily runs across the airfield,

190
00:15:21,181 --> 00:15:24,180
eyeballing for FOD.

191
00:15:24,181 --> 00:15:27,180
But they have to be quick about it.

192
00:15:27,181 --> 00:15:30,180
With planes taking off and landing every two minutes,

193
00:15:30,181 --> 00:15:32,180
there's not a very large window of opportunity

194
00:15:32,181 --> 00:15:36,180
before the inspector himself becomes a piece of FOD.

195
00:15:36,181 --> 00:15:38,180
We're going about 100 right now.

196
00:15:38,181 --> 00:15:40,180
We're nearing the end of the runway here.

197
00:15:40,181 --> 00:15:43,180
Everything looks good up until now.

198
00:15:43,181 --> 00:15:46,180
Okay, runway inspection is complete.

199
00:15:47,180 --> 00:15:55,420
One of the most dangerous, common, and least publicized hazards to runway safety is posed

200
00:15:55,421 --> 00:15:57,420
by birds.

201
00:15:57,421 --> 00:16:04,340
Meet Elvis, a falcon that patrols the skies around McConnell Air Force Base in Wichita,

202
00:16:04,341 --> 00:16:10,800
Kansas, keeping the airspace safe for planes by terrifying other birds.

203
00:16:10,801 --> 00:16:15,060
Falcons are very effective on some species of birds, especially these smaller birds that

204
00:16:15,061 --> 00:16:22,060
out here, gulls, they all recognize him as a natural predator and they all just leave

205
00:16:22,061 --> 00:16:23,060
the area.

206
00:16:23,061 --> 00:16:29,500
A handful of airports have resorted to falcons, including JFK, which has one of the most serious

207
00:16:29,501 --> 00:16:36,000
bird problems in the country and employs no less than 15 falcons to help clear the skies.

208
00:16:36,001 --> 00:16:42,220
McConnell, which sits beneath a major migrating route for geese, ducks and other waterfowl,

209
00:16:42,221 --> 00:16:48,820
about 100 bird strikes a year. Overall in the U.S., no less than 43,000 bird strikes

210
00:16:48,821 --> 00:16:54,576
with civil aircraft have been reported since the FAA began keeping records in 1990.

211
00:16:54,577 --> 00:16:55,277
Officials

212
00:16:55,278 --> 00:17:00,120
believe that most bird strikes are unreported, and that the actual number is five times that

213
00:17:00,121 --> 00:17:00,821
high.

214
00:17:00,822 --> 00:17:08,120
Total damage from bird strikes to commercial air carriers each year worldwide is $1.2 billion.

215
00:17:08,121 --> 00:17:18,120
Every airport that has birds in the vicinity must contend with the likelihood that birds will fly through the operating airspace

216
00:17:18,121 --> 00:17:27,120
and present a hazard to aircraft on landing and approach, either by being ingested in the engine

217
00:17:27,121 --> 00:17:34,120
or striking the aircraft windscreen or surface and causing deformity and damage to the aircraft.

218
00:17:34,121 --> 00:17:41,120
There are many reasons for the dramatic rise in bird plane collisions in recent years.

219
00:17:41,121 --> 00:17:47,840
For one thing, jet engines are more powerful than ever and can suck up just about anything

220
00:17:47,841 --> 00:17:49,720
that crosses their path.

221
00:17:49,721 --> 00:17:53,920
They're also quieter than ever, making it harder for birds to detect aircraft and get

222
00:17:53,921 --> 00:17:55,540
out of their way.

223
00:17:55,541 --> 00:18:01,360
For another, the population levels of many once rare species have exploded in the U.S.,

224
00:18:01,361 --> 00:18:03,520
especially around marshland and open fields,

225
00:18:03,940 --> 00:18:07,000
just the kind of terrain that airports frequently border.

226
00:18:08,120 --> 00:18:12,360
90% of all bird strikes are with birds protected by federal law,

227
00:18:12,780 --> 00:18:16,380
like red-tailed hawks, Canada geese, and sandhill cranes.

228
00:18:17,200 --> 00:18:20,080
Probably the most significant in terms of damage

229
00:18:20,081 --> 00:18:21,440
are the ones that go into the engines.

230
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:23,380
We took a bird in one of the engines,

231
00:18:23,840 --> 00:18:25,560
and we were too far down the runway to stop,

232
00:18:25,561 --> 00:18:27,760
so we went on around and came back and stopped,

233
00:18:27,880 --> 00:18:29,640
only to discover, upon the shutdown,

234
00:18:29,641 --> 00:18:32,280
about a quarter million dollars worth of damage to the blades.

235
00:18:33,220 --> 00:18:34,460
Now that's significant.

236
00:18:34,980 --> 00:18:37,800
What was significant to me was that the engine was still running.

237
00:18:38,620 --> 00:18:39,700
And I was happy with that.

238
00:18:40,280 --> 00:18:42,380
The bird strike problem is so serious

239
00:18:42,381 --> 00:18:44,920
that both the military and engine manufacturers

240
00:18:44,921 --> 00:18:47,920
have been testing aircraft since the 1970s,

241
00:18:48,360 --> 00:18:51,300
firing dead chickens and other fowl at windshields

242
00:18:51,301 --> 00:18:53,120
and into the engines of new jets.

243
00:18:53,760 --> 00:18:56,460
The military won't buy an F-16, for example,

244
00:18:56,461 --> 00:19:01,500
unless it can withstand a direct hit from a four-pound bird at 550 knots.

245
00:19:02,600 --> 00:19:04,700
The U.S. Department of Agriculture, meanwhile,

246
00:19:04,940 --> 00:19:07,200
has an entire research facility near Cleveland

247
00:19:07,201 --> 00:19:10,820
dedicated to coming up with new weapons to wield against birds.

248
00:19:11,900 --> 00:19:13,780
This laser holds some promise,

249
00:19:14,620 --> 00:19:16,480
nudging birds gently out of harm's way

250
00:19:16,481 --> 00:19:18,740
with a beam that birds are afraid to cross.

251
00:19:21,240 --> 00:19:23,900
As do these high-intensity pulsating lights

252
00:19:23,901 --> 00:19:26,340
that could soon be fixed to the wings of aircraft.

253
00:19:26,460 --> 00:19:33,460
The lights would scatter birds just quickly enough for planes and jets to make it through

254
00:19:33,461 --> 00:19:35,460
without a strike.

255
00:19:35,461 --> 00:19:40,120
Until the new tools are available, falcons like Elvis will continue to play a crucial

256
00:19:40,121 --> 00:19:42,660
role at McConnell and other airstrips.

257
00:19:42,661 --> 00:19:47,460
These birds basically establish their territory out here.

258
00:19:47,461 --> 00:19:51,560
If they're flown every day, the resident populations of birds here start thinking, well, there's

259
00:19:51,561 --> 00:19:55,560
a falcon that lives out there, and so they subsequently stay away.

260
00:19:55,561 --> 00:19:58,860
He cost about a dollar a day to feed.

261
00:19:58,861 --> 00:20:03,360
He's about, oh, anywhere from a thousand to two thousand dollars to buy.

262
00:20:03,361 --> 00:20:08,560
There's a lot of hours of time and training to go in to get him where he's at.

263
00:20:08,561 --> 00:20:13,260
He's only flown out here for a little over a month now, so he's just getting started by,

264
00:20:13,261 --> 00:20:15,427
within another two months he'll be cooking out here.

265
00:20:15,428 --> 00:20:16,760
He'll chase everything in sight.

266
00:20:16,761 --> 00:20:20,760
Helping Elvis at McConnell is an English setter

267
00:20:20,761 --> 00:20:21,461
named Pepper.

268
00:20:21,462 --> 00:20:25,360
Her job is to flush birds out of the grass that grows around the airfield.

269
00:20:25,361 --> 00:20:29,360
They get along great. Pepper's a good girl.

270
00:20:29,361 --> 00:20:33,360
She loves it out here. She could run all day.

271
00:20:33,361 --> 00:20:37,360
She never gets tired. Elvis, on the other hand, is a little more temperamental.

272
00:20:37,361 --> 00:20:41,360
They were raised together and just get along fine.

273
00:20:45,360 --> 00:20:49,360
Bird strikes are such a serious and escalating problem

274
00:20:49,361 --> 00:20:53,360
that one entrepreneurial falconer in Canada has developed a fully functioning

275
00:20:53,361 --> 00:20:58,720
Falcon robot that could be cheaper than live birds, since it doesn't have to be trained or maintained.

276
00:21:01,360 --> 00:21:06,880
Robo Falcon can fly for 8 to 12 minutes, has a wingspan of between 4 and 9 feet,

277
00:21:07,320 --> 00:21:10,640
and a wingbeat rate of 3.5 to 7 beats per second.

278
00:21:11,980 --> 00:21:16,420
JFK, among others, has auditioned the mechanical predator, which comes in five sizes,

279
00:21:16,421 --> 00:21:19,000
costs between $6,000 to $10,000

280
00:21:19,001 --> 00:21:22,800
and could one day make all the difference

281
00:21:22,801 --> 00:21:25,980
in the war for airspace between birds like this

282
00:21:25,981 --> 00:21:28,400
and birds like this.

283
00:21:33,680 --> 00:21:36,660
Some of the most fascinating and unusual runways

284
00:21:36,661 --> 00:21:39,440
can be found on America's 12 aircraft carriers,

285
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,780
floating airports that can race to any hotspot in the world

286
00:21:42,781 --> 00:21:44,180
at over 30 knots.

287
00:21:46,420 --> 00:21:50,420
These behemoths carry a fleet of up to 80 planes,

288
00:21:50,421 --> 00:21:53,420
but their runways are just 400 feet long,

289
00:21:53,421 --> 00:21:57,420
stretching out across barely half the deck of the aircraft carrier.

290
00:21:57,421 --> 00:22:01,420
Space, to say the least, is at a premium.

291
00:22:01,421 --> 00:22:04,420
We actually take an entire city,

292
00:22:04,421 --> 00:22:06,420
a city at sea, an airport at sea,

293
00:22:06,421 --> 00:22:10,420
and we condense it down into that ship,

294
00:22:10,421 --> 00:22:15,420
which is 1,100 feet long, and it's quite an operation.

295
00:22:15,421 --> 00:22:19,640
It's a real ballet of things going on all at the same time.

296
00:22:21,200 --> 00:22:23,880
Aircraft carrier runways aren't long enough for jets,

297
00:22:24,080 --> 00:22:27,780
which weigh up to 65,000 pounds, to lift off on their own power.

298
00:22:28,300 --> 00:22:30,380
So they have to be catapulted into flight.

299
00:22:32,140 --> 00:22:35,040
Each carrier has four steam-powered catapults,

300
00:22:35,620 --> 00:22:40,040
which attach by way of a slide track on the runway to a T-bar on the plane.

301
00:22:40,860 --> 00:22:44,320
The hydraulic engines that power the catapult are below the runway

302
00:22:44,321 --> 00:22:46,620
and weigh a million pounds apiece.

303
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,560
Each one is capable of launching an aircraft

304
00:22:50,561 --> 00:22:53,940
from zero to 150 miles an hour in under two seconds.

305
00:22:54,460 --> 00:22:56,340
The catapults are 300 feet long

306
00:22:56,341 --> 00:22:58,900
and are comprised of two steel cylinders

307
00:22:58,901 --> 00:23:02,280
with two 2,500-pound pistons inside.

308
00:23:02,940 --> 00:23:06,140
These catapults can create an amazing amount of force

309
00:23:06,141 --> 00:23:08,500
and will launch the aircraft off the end of the flight deck

310
00:23:08,501 --> 00:23:11,240
regardless of the fact that the engines are on or not.

311
00:23:11,241 --> 00:23:16,180
Your head is back against the headrest

312
00:23:16,181 --> 00:23:18,780
because the G-forces will take your head

313
00:23:18,781 --> 00:23:20,340
and put it back against the seat.

314
00:23:20,980 --> 00:23:23,080
And then as soon as you're airborne,

315
00:23:23,660 --> 00:23:25,260
you kind of get this pause

316
00:23:25,261 --> 00:23:27,600
because now the acceleration has stopped,

317
00:23:27,940 --> 00:23:30,540
and now the only thing that's driving the airplane forward

318
00:23:30,541 --> 00:23:32,300
is the aircraft's two engines.

319
00:23:34,560 --> 00:23:37,260
Landing on a runway at sea is even more hair-raising,

320
00:23:37,261 --> 00:23:40,360
a kind of controlled crash at very close quarters.

321
00:23:41,240 --> 00:23:45,340
Once again, the runway is too short for a plane to land in a conventional manner.

322
00:23:46,140 --> 00:23:50,140
Instead, a hook on the tail of the plane has to catch one of four arresting cables

323
00:23:50,141 --> 00:23:52,040
that are stretched out across the deck.

324
00:23:53,480 --> 00:23:59,220
It is a pretty violent stop when you're going from 140 miles an hour down to zero

325
00:23:59,221 --> 00:24:01,920
in a matter of about 450 feet.

326
00:24:02,520 --> 00:24:06,140
One of the training airplanes I flew when I first started flying,

327
00:24:06,660 --> 00:24:08,980
you would get black and blue marks right here

328
00:24:08,981 --> 00:24:11,100
when you caught the arresting gear

329
00:24:11,101 --> 00:24:13,600
and your body wants to keep flying forward,

330
00:24:13,720 --> 00:24:16,100
but the airplane's going to stop when you caught a wire.

331
00:24:17,120 --> 00:24:19,080
Making the landing even more violent

332
00:24:19,081 --> 00:24:22,420
is the fact that these pilots have to gun their engines on touchdown,

333
00:24:22,980 --> 00:24:24,480
just in case they miss the wire

334
00:24:24,481 --> 00:24:26,680
and have to stay airborne for a second go-round.

335
00:24:27,760 --> 00:24:30,520
They also have to land exactly on center line.

336
00:24:31,060 --> 00:24:34,200
The lateral margin of error for these runways is just 5 feet.

337
00:24:35,200 --> 00:24:36,420
Any more than that,

338
00:24:36,421 --> 00:24:40,180
and the larger jets could clip the wings of an adjacent jet readying for takeoff.

339
00:24:41,140 --> 00:24:45,540
Oh yeah, did we forget to mention that these pilots have to do all this with a moving target?

340
00:24:46,620 --> 00:24:49,180
We need a minimum amount of wind over the flight deck.

341
00:24:49,640 --> 00:24:53,620
However, we're frequently out in the Persian Gulf and there's no wind at all,

342
00:24:53,680 --> 00:24:55,200
and the carrier has to create that wind.

343
00:24:55,540 --> 00:24:59,880
So the carrier will be going 25, sometimes 30 knots through the water.

344
00:25:00,400 --> 00:25:04,620
From the perspective of the pilot orbiting overhead the carrier in preparation for landing,

345
00:25:04,621 --> 00:25:09,620
He's looking down watching the carrier cruise through the water 25 knots.

346
00:25:09,621 --> 00:25:13,620
It almost looks unreal that he's now going to land that plane on it.

347
00:25:13,621 --> 00:25:15,620
You can imagine the type of precision it takes to fly that,

348
00:25:15,621 --> 00:25:18,620
and then when they come out of the fog or the clouds

349
00:25:18,621 --> 00:25:21,620
and see the carrier to make those last few corrections precisely

350
00:25:21,621 --> 00:25:24,620
and land safely on the ship.

351
00:25:28,620 --> 00:25:32,620
Not surprisingly, the pilots don't always manage to land.

352
00:25:32,621 --> 00:25:39,620
The average success rate for catching a cable is about 90%.

353
00:25:39,621 --> 00:25:47,620
Sometimes pilots catch the wire, but the hooking mechanism itself goes awry.

354
00:25:47,621 --> 00:25:53,620
Which is why the Navy is always interested in testing other specialized landing gear,

355
00:25:53,621 --> 00:25:59,620
like this arresting web.

356
00:25:59,621 --> 00:26:03,800
The idea of rigging of military vessels so that you can launch planes at sea

357
00:26:03,801 --> 00:26:06,340
goes back to the earliest days of aviation.

358
00:26:07,880 --> 00:26:11,960
A daredevil pilot flew a biplane off a warship in 1910,

359
00:26:12,580 --> 00:26:16,820
and a decade later, the U.S. Navy converted a coal ship with a wooden deck

360
00:26:16,821 --> 00:26:20,680
into the USS Langley, America's first aircraft carrier.

361
00:26:22,400 --> 00:26:26,580
Planes of the period were light enough to launch off the deck without the use of catapults,

362
00:26:26,581 --> 00:26:29,540
although launching devices were introduced in 1915.

363
00:26:29,620 --> 00:26:34,620
But arresting cables were critical from the start in bringing aircraft to a stop.

364
00:26:34,621 --> 00:26:38,620
The first team catapults were tested in 1934,

365
00:26:38,621 --> 00:26:43,620
and they were barely powerful enough to keep planes aloft until they could fly under their own power.

366
00:26:43,621 --> 00:26:48,620
By the start of World War II, aircraft carriers were devastating war machines,

367
00:26:48,621 --> 00:26:54,620
each one packed with a fleet of bombers, and they played a critical role, especially in the Pacific.

368
00:26:54,621 --> 00:27:01,620
The Cold War spurred the development of aircraft carrier technology, and steam catapults capable

369
00:27:01,621 --> 00:27:05,120
of launching jets were introduced in 1954.

370
00:27:05,121 --> 00:27:08,620
By now, America's airport in the ocean was

371
00:27:08,621 --> 00:27:11,836
a forbidding symbol of power and reach.

372
00:27:11,837 --> 00:27:16,620
Although there were occasional failures, like this botched

373
00:27:16,621 --> 00:27:21,620
landing on the carrier Essex in 1956.

374
00:27:24,620 --> 00:27:30,700
Keeping those failures to a minimum today has a lot to do with paying constant attention

375
00:27:30,701 --> 00:27:32,780
to the surface of these cruising airstrips.

376
00:27:34,320 --> 00:27:38,380
Skid problems are a huge concern, more so than on conventional runways,

377
00:27:38,381 --> 00:27:44,040
since the aircraft carrier exists in an all-wet environment that can pitch and roll at any time.

378
00:27:45,680 --> 00:27:49,960
We've seen waves that are breaking over the flight deck, 60 feet tall waves,

379
00:27:49,961 --> 00:27:54,820
sometimes where the bow is pitching down plus or minus 20 feet.

380
00:27:55,260 --> 00:27:59,040
So we need to have an environment where the planes can move safely on the flight deck

381
00:27:59,041 --> 00:28:00,340
and provide good traction.

382
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:06,660
Every 18 months, the steel-plated runway is resurfaced with non-skid,

383
00:28:07,140 --> 00:28:09,680
a texturized coating made up of metal and sand.

384
00:28:10,160 --> 00:28:15,860
The application of this special surface takes weeks to put on.

385
00:28:16,140 --> 00:28:17,860
It has to be stripped down to bare metal.

386
00:28:17,861 --> 00:28:24,640
And then once the environmental conditions are right, we will lay this down with very specialized paintbrushes to ensure that the grain is correct.

387
00:28:25,060 --> 00:28:29,260
And it takes about four days to cure and make to the required hardness.

388
00:28:29,360 --> 00:28:35,220
So this will stand up to the pounding of aircraft and the tailhooks slamming down on the surface.

389
00:28:36,160 --> 00:28:38,540
The slamming of the tailhooks is incessant.

390
00:28:38,920 --> 00:28:46,720
The maximum tempo or rate of takeoffs and landings on an aircraft carrier is two takeoffs and one landing every 37 seconds during the day.

391
00:28:46,721 --> 00:28:50,600
and one takeoff and landing every minute at night.

392
00:28:55,640 --> 00:28:58,960
America's space shuttle goes up higher and stays there longer

393
00:28:58,961 --> 00:29:02,100
than any other contemporary manned aircraft in the world.

394
00:29:03,140 --> 00:29:05,900
But the shuttle, like everything else that leaves the ground,

395
00:29:06,240 --> 00:29:07,640
needs a place to touch down.

396
00:29:09,760 --> 00:29:14,040
The runway itself, of course, was desired to be incredibly flat,

397
00:29:14,360 --> 00:29:16,280
incredibly long and wide.

398
00:29:16,720 --> 00:29:19,720
because you do have, at the time the runway was designed,

399
00:29:20,440 --> 00:29:23,760
essentially an untested vehicle,

400
00:29:24,100 --> 00:29:27,260
and it's not going to handle quite as nicely as an airliner,

401
00:29:27,380 --> 00:29:31,120
so you want a very long runway, very flat, very wide.

402
00:29:32,200 --> 00:29:35,480
The shuttle actually has two runways in the U.S.,

403
00:29:35,481 --> 00:29:38,600
the main one at Kennedy Space Center in Cape Canaveral, Florida,

404
00:29:38,900 --> 00:29:42,120
and a backup at Edwards Air Force Base in Southern California.

405
00:29:42,121 --> 00:29:46,920
The runway at the Kennedy Space Center is one of the longest in the world.

406
00:29:47,720 --> 00:29:52,140
15,000 feet long, with 1,000-foot overruns on each end.

407
00:29:52,700 --> 00:29:56,560
That's nearly three miles, or 50 football fields end to end.

408
00:29:57,840 --> 00:30:02,480
It's also 3,000 feet longer than the runways at JFK and LAX.

409
00:30:03,680 --> 00:30:08,980
The shuttle needs all that room because its braking system isn't comparable to that of conventional jets.

410
00:30:08,981 --> 00:30:14,860
Jets shift their engines into reverse thrust to slow themselves down.

411
00:30:15,520 --> 00:30:17,280
But the shuttle doesn't have jet engines.

412
00:30:17,840 --> 00:30:20,560
It's launched by rocket power and glides home.

413
00:30:21,200 --> 00:30:27,380
And so it has to rely entirely on wheel brakes, a drag chute, gravity, and a whole lot of runway room.

414
00:30:29,240 --> 00:30:36,540
NASA has been conducting runway tests at its aircraft landing dynamics facility in Hampton, Virginia, since the mid-1950s.

415
00:30:38,980 --> 00:30:43,140
Here, the hydraulically powered slide track can simulate shuttle touchdowns,

416
00:30:43,720 --> 00:30:48,420
testing tires and runway surfaces at the same stresses as at the Kennedy Space Center.

417
00:30:50,580 --> 00:30:56,360
We use a carriage, a tubular steel carriage, that weighs almost 60 tons.

418
00:30:57,140 --> 00:31:01,840
To get it up to speed, we fire a jet of water at the rear of this carriage

419
00:31:01,841 --> 00:31:06,100
and accelerate it from zero up to 250 miles an hour in just about two seconds.

420
00:31:06,101 --> 00:31:10,100
There's about 20 G's of acceleration during that process.

421
00:31:10,101 --> 00:31:15,100
And then once we get the carriage up to speed, it coasts down the remainder of a half-mile-long runway.

422
00:31:15,101 --> 00:31:22,100
And during that time, we apply the vertical loads that the tire would see during a true orbiter landing.

423
00:31:22,101 --> 00:31:26,100
Tires are a critical piece of the shuttle runway equation,

424
00:31:26,101 --> 00:31:31,100
even more so than with commercial jets, which are actually much heavier.

425
00:31:31,101 --> 00:31:35,100
That's because the stress per tire on landing is much greater for the shuttle.

426
00:31:35,101 --> 00:31:43,100
A 747 has four tires per main landing gear, whereas the orbiter has two tires per gear.

427
00:31:43,101 --> 00:31:54,100
But because of the aerodynamics of the shuttle, the individual tire loads go sometimes two to three times the individual tire loads on, say, a 747.

428
00:31:54,101 --> 00:32:01,100
In terms of pounds, you might see 50,000 pounds on a single tire on a 747 aircraft.

429
00:32:01,101 --> 00:32:02,042
aircraft.

430
00:32:02,043 --> 00:32:10,100
For the orbiter, you can see individual tire loads as high as 140,000 pounds.

431
00:32:10,101 --> 00:32:19,100
The stress is so great that the $4,000 16-ply shuttle tires can be used safely just six times,

432
00:32:19,101 --> 00:32:24,350
in contrast to the $800 747 tires, which can touch down 250 times.

433
00:32:24,351 --> 00:32:26,100
In fact, shuttle tires

434
00:32:26,101 --> 00:32:28,671
tires are only used once.

435
00:32:28,672 --> 00:32:35,460
Why jeopardize a $2 billion vehicle plus a crew for a $4,000 tire?

436
00:32:37,620 --> 00:32:42,620
Like the tire, the surface of this runway also has to be able to handle the phenomenal stress

437
00:32:42,621 --> 00:32:46,587
loads of touchdown, especially in bad weather.

438
00:32:46,588 --> 00:32:50,640
Given the higher landing speeds and the heavier

439
00:32:50,641 --> 00:32:55,120
weight per tire, concern about skidding was so great when the runway was first built

440
00:32:55,121 --> 00:32:58,680
that it was initially coated with an astoundingly rough surface.

441
00:32:59,200 --> 00:33:02,340
The texture of the runway when it was originally designed and built

442
00:33:02,341 --> 00:33:07,740
was the most aggressive, rough texture of any runway that we're aware of in the world.

443
00:33:07,741 --> 00:33:11,080
It was, again, purposefully designed that way

444
00:33:11,081 --> 00:33:15,200
to combat rainfall rates as high as 3 inches an hour,

445
00:33:15,560 --> 00:33:18,200
which would flood any other runway in the world.

446
00:33:18,860 --> 00:33:22,480
This is a cheese grater surface with really aggressive transverse grooving

447
00:33:22,481 --> 00:33:24,840
and very, very rough surfaces.

448
00:33:25,120 --> 00:33:29,440
This is great for wet runway performance, but it's terrible for wear.

449
00:33:30,140 --> 00:33:33,940
The problem was the surface was so rough it was chewing up the tires,

450
00:33:34,400 --> 00:33:37,920
especially when any kind of crosswind was pushing the tires against the grain.

451
00:33:38,960 --> 00:33:44,560
Within the first five landings, we would see tires that had some of their carcass cords showing.

452
00:33:45,080 --> 00:33:50,820
There's no other airplane in the world that would perform a single landing in relatively benign conditions

453
00:33:50,821 --> 00:33:56,200
and yet have wear into the structure of the tire.

454
00:33:57,160 --> 00:34:00,540
After many experiments, Langley toned down the runway surface,

455
00:34:00,920 --> 00:34:04,640
coming up with just the right balance between anti-skid and wear and tear.

456
00:34:06,160 --> 00:34:09,100
Because of the extreme levels of touchdown stress,

457
00:34:09,300 --> 00:34:14,820
the shuttle runway has to be one of the toughest, most stable and crack-resistant runways in the world,

458
00:34:15,680 --> 00:34:19,460
which is amazing considering that it was built on Florida swampland

459
00:34:19,461 --> 00:34:24,060
and had to be solidified with compressed earth dredged from nearby canals.

460
00:34:25,300 --> 00:34:29,380
That foundation is topped by a 6-inch layer of soil-cement mixture,

461
00:34:29,800 --> 00:34:32,980
which is in turn buried under 16 inches of solid concrete,

462
00:34:33,880 --> 00:34:37,980
all of which is maintained at what is virtually a 0% grade.

463
00:34:40,980 --> 00:34:46,880
The shuttle runway is built to much higher standards in terms of flatness and levelness

464
00:34:46,881 --> 00:34:49,720
compared to the average runway at a commercial airport.

465
00:34:50,340 --> 00:34:55,660
All of us have seen long period dips and bumps in those runways.

466
00:34:56,240 --> 00:34:59,020
The flatness of the shuttle runway is pretty incredible.

467
00:34:59,200 --> 00:35:02,040
It's approximately two-tenths of an inch per 1,000 feet,

468
00:35:02,340 --> 00:35:03,620
which is very, very flat.

469
00:35:04,520 --> 00:35:08,480
And when you consider that it's in the area that it's built in,

470
00:35:08,481 --> 00:35:14,300
sort of a swampy area, you know, with ground motion and so forth,

471
00:35:14,301 --> 00:35:18,000
It took quite a bit to make it that flat and keep it that flat.

472
00:35:19,140 --> 00:35:25,140
Speaking of flat, one of Langley's worst nightmares involves landing the shuttle with a blown tire.

473
00:35:26,160 --> 00:35:31,180
In 1989, researchers tackled the question of where to land the shuttle in the event of a blowout.

474
00:35:31,840 --> 00:35:36,860
On the rough concrete of Kennedy, or the softer, wide-open lake bed of Edwards Air Force Base.

475
00:35:37,720 --> 00:35:39,080
There's two schools of thought.

476
00:35:39,081 --> 00:35:43,000
One is land on the lake bed because it is wide open.

477
00:35:43,001 --> 00:35:45,580
And you do have a lot of room for problems.

478
00:35:46,220 --> 00:35:53,840
But the concern there may be, are you going to dig into that surface and cause so much drag that you lose control of the vehicle?

479
00:35:55,320 --> 00:36:00,160
On the other side of the coin, if you're on a concrete runway, we've done tests here at our facility,

480
00:36:00,720 --> 00:36:04,940
that have demonstrated the fact that you can adequately control the vehicle.

481
00:36:05,180 --> 00:36:06,580
Friction won't be too high.

482
00:36:06,900 --> 00:36:12,700
But you're likely to get a tremendous amount of damage because you're grinding through your tire, your wheel, your brake stack.

483
00:36:13,000 --> 00:36:20,000
After rigging up small-scale tests at Langley, researchers decided to stage a much more realistic shuttle blowout,

484
00:36:20,001 --> 00:36:25,000
using a Convair 990 with a shuttle tire and landing gear mounted in its belly.

485
00:36:25,001 --> 00:36:34,000
The idea was in fact to blow the tire up on purpose and continue the test and drag the wheel and all the parts down the runway

486
00:36:34,001 --> 00:36:37,000
and measure the drag forces associated with that.

487
00:36:43,000 --> 00:36:44,660
We're on the hook. We're on the hook.

488
00:36:44,900 --> 00:36:45,600
We're on the tire.

489
00:36:45,820 --> 00:36:47,740
Stop this. Stop this. Stop this.

490
00:36:49,160 --> 00:36:49,920
Reverse on.

491
00:36:51,280 --> 00:36:56,160
Once it blows up, you see this tremendous ball of flame behind the aircraft

492
00:36:56,161 --> 00:36:58,540
that doesn't stop until they pick the tire up off the ground.

493
00:37:00,420 --> 00:37:04,800
The flame is caused by a volatile combination of rubber and aluminum dust

494
00:37:04,801 --> 00:37:08,140
that gets kicked up as the tire and wheelbase scrape the concrete.

495
00:37:09,000 --> 00:37:13,060
Aluminum and rubber just happen to be two key ingredients of rocket fuel.

496
00:37:14,700 --> 00:37:21,720
Despite the damage, however, the tests proved that landing on concrete at Kennedy is controllable and relatively safe.

497
00:37:22,340 --> 00:37:25,520
And that's where the shuttle will touch down if it ever has a flat.

498
00:37:29,480 --> 00:37:33,120
Most aviation takes place in and around urban areas,

499
00:37:33,480 --> 00:37:38,100
where runways are hefty pieces of infrastructure that have been designed and constructed with great care.

500
00:37:38,140 --> 00:37:44,940
For those who like to fly on the wild side, however, runways aren't quite so reassuring.

501
00:37:52,440 --> 00:37:57,160
Welcome to the Frank Church River of No Return Wilderness in central Idaho.

502
00:37:57,740 --> 00:38:04,960
4.6 million acres of canyons and gorges where flying is a thrill and landing can seem unthinkable.

503
00:38:08,140 --> 00:38:15,140
Lori McNichol has been flying bush planes in Idaho's backcountry for 20 years.

504
00:38:15,141 --> 00:38:20,140
We have over 48 different airstrips that we can land at.

505
00:38:20,141 --> 00:38:24,140
And these airstrips are in the heart of the wilderness.

506
00:38:24,141 --> 00:38:29,140
They are cut out of just the side of the hill.

507
00:38:29,141 --> 00:38:35,140
And when you are traveling across this vast land, which 4.6 million acres is,

508
00:38:35,141 --> 00:38:39,540
you are looking for one little scratch,

509
00:38:39,541 --> 00:38:41,220
or a lot of people say,

510
00:38:41,580 --> 00:38:44,280
that postage stamp is where we're going.

511
00:38:49,320 --> 00:38:53,060
Each landing area has some type of gotcha,

512
00:38:53,500 --> 00:38:57,060
and it may look great when you're circling overhead,

513
00:38:57,280 --> 00:38:58,540
but when you turn final,

514
00:38:59,080 --> 00:39:02,040
and you have to tell yourself that you're committed,

515
00:39:02,360 --> 00:39:04,300
that there's simply no go around here,

516
00:39:04,301 --> 00:39:07,860
You have to be ready, and you never know what you're going to get.

517
00:39:08,640 --> 00:39:12,360
That's partly because what you're going to get is 100% natural.

518
00:39:13,380 --> 00:39:17,920
These runways are made out of the grass, dirt, and gravel that were found on the site.

519
00:39:18,860 --> 00:39:22,580
In fact, it's against the law to bring outside materials into this wilderness,

520
00:39:23,220 --> 00:39:26,760
or even to move found elements like gravel from one spot to another.

521
00:39:29,120 --> 00:39:31,880
Indeed, it's even against the law to build runways here,

522
00:39:31,881 --> 00:39:41,952
And most of these airstrips date from the 1930s and 40s, when pilots and backwoodsmen fashioned them entirely by hand, pulling out stumps and rocks and cutting down

523
00:39:41,953 --> 00:39:42,653
trees.

524
00:39:43,100 --> 00:39:49,160
They are maintained today without the use of any mechanical tools that might disrupt or seem out of place in this protected environment.

525
00:39:49,940 --> 00:39:51,300
Even wheelbarrows are banned.

526
00:39:53,160 --> 00:39:55,980
A little bit on the flat side, looking pretty good.

527
00:39:56,700 --> 00:40:00,560
I'm looking at that spot that I like, lining up at the end of the runway.

528
00:40:00,561 --> 00:40:09,440
The Soldier Bar Runway in the heart of the River of No Return Wilderness is 1,600 feet long and 4,200 feet above sea level.

529
00:40:14,500 --> 00:40:17,740
The surface consists of grass and embedded rock.

530
00:40:18,420 --> 00:40:23,400
Rubber strips wedged in by hand act as water bars to prevent erosion caused by rain.

531
00:40:23,401 --> 00:40:30,400
This runway is called Cabin Creek because it actually rests on a dried out creek bed

532
00:40:30,401 --> 00:40:36,400
that was painstakingly cleared of everything except the hard pan gravel on which planes now land.

533
00:40:40,400 --> 00:40:45,400
There are thousands of bush pilots flying into and out of hard to access parts of the U.S.

534
00:40:45,401 --> 00:40:47,400
as well as the rest of the world.

535
00:40:47,401 --> 00:40:50,300
of the world.

536
00:40:50,301 --> 00:40:54,800
Alaska is still the bush pilot center of the world, with some flyers bringing everything

537
00:40:54,801 --> 00:41:02,640
from groceries to mail to tourists to otherwise inaccessible communities.

538
00:41:02,641 --> 00:41:11,320
And oftentimes landing on improvised runways made out of packed snow and ice.

539
00:41:11,321 --> 00:41:14,060
Florida is another haven for bush pilots.

540
00:41:14,061 --> 00:41:18,980
here the fliers specialize in taking off and touching down on water, much like aviators

541
00:41:18,981 --> 00:41:22,835
did with the boat planes of the 1920s and 30s.

542
00:41:22,836 --> 00:41:26,020
Water, surprisingly, is trickiest when

543
00:41:26,021 --> 00:41:29,420
it's calmest, and the glassy surface is hard to read.

544
00:41:30,140 --> 00:41:36,000
You have no depth whatsoever, so that when you set up your descent profile and you try

545
00:41:36,001 --> 00:41:42,600
to focus on something, you really try hard to have some type of land or shore in view,

546
00:41:42,601 --> 00:41:46,600
but very often you don't, so you're waiting for that water.

547
00:41:47,180 --> 00:41:54,700
And it's very, very important to make this landing in quite a stick-back fashion.

548
00:41:57,640 --> 00:42:01,360
Bush runways are in fact the most dangerous airstrips in the world.

549
00:42:02,500 --> 00:42:07,760
Every year in Idaho alone, some half-dozen pilots die trying to bring their planes down.

550
00:42:07,761 --> 00:42:14,760
But for backcountry flyers, the danger is part of the appeal.

551
00:42:14,761 --> 00:42:23,760
The reason why we're all driven is because of the challenge of each runway that we approach.

552
00:42:23,761 --> 00:42:29,760
Each and every runway with its own set of gotchas and its whole new look.

553
00:42:29,761 --> 00:42:35,760
You can go to that same strip day after day and it has something different to give you.

554
00:42:35,761 --> 00:42:38,360
and that's what we love about it.

555
00:42:38,520 --> 00:42:40,360
It always keeps us on our toes.

556
00:42:43,220 --> 00:42:46,720
On our toes probably isn't the way most of us want to feel

557
00:42:46,721 --> 00:42:49,300
when we're about to take off or land in a 747.

558
00:42:50,540 --> 00:42:53,200
The runway is one part of the flying experience

559
00:42:53,201 --> 00:42:55,360
that we expect to have a lot of confidence in,

560
00:42:55,660 --> 00:42:56,640
and for good reason.

561
00:42:57,520 --> 00:42:59,080
These strips of terra firma,

562
00:42:59,500 --> 00:43:02,240
whether they be made of concrete or riverbed gravel,

563
00:43:02,440 --> 00:43:04,920
are an essential part of the flying equation.

564
00:43:05,760 --> 00:43:08,580
which is the paradox that lies at the heart of aviation.

565
00:43:09,140 --> 00:43:13,860
In order to get up into the air, you need to have a very firm foot on the ground.

